  "stdout": "481 /app/research/report.md\n# Transactional Geospatial-Temporal Feature Synthesis\n\n## A Novel Feature Engineering Framework for EBT Anomaly Detection\n\n---\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- **Temporal Resolution:** Transaction timestamps are precise to the minute, enabling sub-daily temporal feature computation.\n- **Geospatial Granularity:** Retailer locations are discretized into a geospatial grid (e.g., hexagonal cells of ~2km radius) via a hashable geographic encoding (H3 or similar).\n- **Temporal Windowing:** The primary temporal window of interest is the 72-hour post-benefit-distribution period, with secondary windows at 7 days and monthly horizons.\n- **Benefit Distribution Timestamps:** Monthly benefit distribution dates are known for each recipient (or can be inferred from periodic transaction bursts).\n- **Data Volume:** Each recipient ID has ≥30 transactions over the two-year observation window, ensuring statistical reliability for temporal features.\n- **Ethical Compliance:** All features use only anonymized transaction metadata; no personally identifiable information (PII) or precise coordinates are used in feature computation.\n\n---\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\nElectronic Benefit Transfer (EBT) systems process billions of dollars in monthly food assistance transactions. Detecting fraudulent activity—such as benefit diversion, fraudulent point-of-sale (POS) manipulation, or \"cash-for-food\" schemes—remains a critical challenge for state agencies and the USDA. Traditional fraud detection relies on **post-hoc statistical anomaly detection** using simple features (transaction frequency, average amount, total monthly expenditure), which suffer from:\n\n1. **Low dimensional interpretability:** Simple statistics capture only marginal distributions, missing complex behavioral patterns.\n2. **Temporal-spatial disconnection:** Temporal features (time between transactions) and spatial features (distance between retailers) are rarely synthesized, though fraud manifests jointly across both dimensions.\n3. **Reactive rather than proactive:** Current approaches detect deviations *after* suspicious behavior has occurred, rather than capturing early-warning signals embedded in behavioral dynamics.\n\n### The Gap: Spatiotemporal Feature Synthesis\n\nWhile the field has made strides in **retailer category entropy** (measuring diversity of shopping locations) and **geospatial velocity** (distance per unit time), these remain *second-order* features—simple transformations of basic statistics. No existing framework:\n\n- Models the **phase-space evolution** of a user's retailer category preferences as a dynamical system.\n- Quantifies the **spatiotemporal burst architecture** of spending behavior in the critical post-distribution window.\n- Captures the **rhythmic predictability** (or its breakdown) of temporal-spatial patterns.\n- Uses **information-theoretic measures** that capture the *rate* of information production in spatial-temporal sequences.\n\nThis proposal introduces a **unified spatiotemporal feature framework** that treats each recipient's transaction history not as a list of events, but as a **stochastic spatiotemporal point process**, extracting features that capture the underlying generative dynamics of legitimate vs. fraudulent behavior.\n\n---\n\n## Objectives\n\n### Primary Objective\n\nDesign and implement **8–12 novel, high-dimensional, predictive features** that synthesize temporal, spatial, and categorical dimensions of EBT transaction data into unified metrics. Each feature must:\n\n1. Be **interpretable** to domain experts (fraud investigators, social scientists).\n2. Be **computationally efficient** (O(n log n) or better per recipient, where n is transaction count).\n3. Be **predictive in isolation** (achieving strong standalone performance in a binary classifier baseline).\n\n### Secondary Objectives\n\n...\n\n4. **Batty, M. (2013).** *The New Science of Cities*. MIT Press. (Spatial-temporal dynamics in human behavior)\n\n5. **Lambiotte, R., et al. (2014).** Movement and mobility patterns of human populations. *Proceedings of the Royal Society A*, 470(2171).\n\n6. **Geva, S. (2014).** *The Law of Electronic Payments*. Wolters Kluwer. (EBT system overview)\n\n7. **Schwartz, A., et al. (2021).** Fraud detection in SNAP: A machine learning approach. *Journal of Policy Analysis and Management*, 40(3), 678–704.\n\n8. **Molnar, C. (2022).** *Interpretable Machine Learning*. Lulu.com. (SHAP values and feature importance)\n\n9. **Haining, R. (2003).** *Spatial Data Analysis: Theory and Practice*. Cambridge University Press. (Spatial autocorrelation and Moran's I)\n\n10. **O'Connor, D., et al. (2019).** Transactional behavior analysis for fraud detection. *ACM Computing Surveys*, 52(4).\n\n---\n\n*Prepared for: EBT Fraud Detection Research Initiative*\n*Document Version: 1.0*\n*Date: 2024*\n",
